#Glioblastoma $NWBO #DCVax #Immunotherapy #SITC2026 #SITC26

Mel lived the first one. In 1998 a Phase 1 trial took a terminal leukemia and turned it into a disease people grow old with. #Gleevec works because CML runs on one broken switch, BCR-ABL, and one pill can jam it.

The common solid tumors never offered one switch. Each carries its own private set of mutations, and when a drug does find a target the tumor rewires around it. That is why there has never been a Gleevec for the cancers that kill most people.

DCVax-L does not chase mutations. The patient's own dendritic cells are loaded with the whole tumor, not a chosen piece of it, and the immune system picks the targets. In newly diagnosed glioblastoma that produced 13% alive at five years (or more) against 5.7% for propensity score matched external controls. A curve with a tail is what immune memory looks like.

It happened in the textbook cold tumor. Few T cells ever reach a glioblastoma, which is why checkpoint drugs failed there in three Phase 3 trials. A checkpoint drug releases the brakes on T cells that already exist. In a cold tumor there is nothing to release. Dendritic cells sit one step upstream. They create those T cells, so the vaccine does not need the tumor to be warm. It is how a tumor gets warm.

And once it is warm, the checkpoint drug that failed alone finally has something to work with. Dr. Liau's UCLA group tested the order in recurrent glioblastoma and reported the result at EANO 2026. In 35 patients, Keytruda before the vaccine meant a median survival of 271 days. Keytruda after the vaccine meant 555 days, and the risk of death fell by two thirds. Tumor and blood showed why: more T cells in the tumor and more interferon and granzyme when the vaccine came first. Same two drugs, same disease. The order alone doubled survival.

The trial proved the platform in glioblastoma. The platform never cared which tumor the lysate came from, and the sequence does not care either. 𝗧𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗲 𝗚𝗹𝗲𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗰 𝗺𝗼𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁, 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝘁𝗶𝗺𝗲 𝗶𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗳𝗼𝗿 𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘆 𝘀𝗼𝗹𝗶𝗱 𝘁𝘂𝗺𝗼𝗿.

