Canada cannot claim to defend human rights while leaving those who expose Beijing’s abuses to face intimidation on Canadian soil. If Ottawa looks away to protect its trading relationship with China, that silence becomes complicity.

At Davos, Prime Minister @MarkJCarney warned that middle powers must work together or find themselves on the menu. But pursuing closer ties with Beijing offers little protection if we allow the Chinese Communist Party to decide who can speak freely in Canada while potentially soft pedaling systemic human rights abuses. 

Dr. Gyal Lo’s case puts that principle to the test. A Canadian citizen who has helped expose China’s residential school system in Tibet, he now faces a Chinese lawsuit that Tibet Action Institute describes as retaliation for his advocacy. 

Legal documents were served on his wife at their Toronto home. This is Lawfare something to which Optimum and the China Democracy Fund fully understand and have effectively defended over the past decade. 

@UN experts have warned that approximately one million Tibetan children are affected by residential school policies aimed at assimilating them culturally, religiously and linguistically. Canada’s own residential school history should make us especially alert to the damage caused when children are separated from their families, language and identity no matter what the good intentions were. Last year Optimum held a press conference to highlight how the CCP had taken the Canadian system and put it on steroids in order to control and sublimate the Tibetan people.  Watch here https://t.co/mdTrZO0kpj

Ottawa must take these allegations of transnational repression seriously, protect Dr. Gyal Lo’s safety and freedom to speak, and make clear to Beijing that commercial relations do not purchase Canadian silence. Our commitment to human rights must extend to people living under Chinese rule and those who seek safety here. @C_DemFoundation 

Join us in Ottawa on November 25 for the Canadian launch of Dr. Hon-Shiang Lau’s Tibet Was Never a Part of China Since Antiquity. Drawing on China’s own official historical records, his work challenges the narrative Beijing uses to justify its control of Tibet.
